1
00:00:17,239 --> 00:00:25,545
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can one know that you're making progress in practice, if your practice is going right?

2
00:00:26,445 --> 00:00:27,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it going wrong?

3
00:00:30,308 --> 00:00:36,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's something related to what we spoke before, being self-transparent and authentic.

4
00:00:36,972 --> 00:00:45,378
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are self-transparent, and by that it's meant if you don't... if you don't lie to yourself, as in, if you don't try to

5
00:00:46,039 --> 00:00:47,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Turn a blind eye to the truth.

6
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:54,724
[SPEAKER_00]: And in this case, in this case, the truth being, you are not free from suffering.

7
00:00:54,784 --> 00:00:56,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody starts as a puthujjana.

8
00:00:57,406 --> 00:00:59,126
[SPEAKER_00]: So that, that should be your criteria.

9
00:00:59,327 --> 00:01:04,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, whatever you're doing, in terms of the practice, it should be freeing you from suffering.

10
00:01:05,890 --> 00:01:06,751
[SPEAKER_00]: At least to a degree.

11
00:01:07,051 --> 00:01:09,652
[SPEAKER_00]: At least to a degree that you can then start discerning.

12
00:01:09,712 --> 00:01:12,934
[SPEAKER_00]: And then use that criteria to keep doing that thing more.

13
00:01:13,995 --> 00:01:14,115
[SPEAKER_00]: But

14
00:01:14,738 --> 00:01:23,920
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason why it won't be so easy is because people are not self-transparent, because that's done for the purpose of protecting oneself from suffering.

15
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:31,002
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't acknowledge certain truths, you don't admit to yourself, you hold a view, you defend a stance point.

16
00:01:31,582 --> 00:01:35,223
[SPEAKER_00]: All that is done so you don't expose yourself to that existential suffering.

17
00:01:36,043 --> 00:01:43,465
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you remove that attitude, and then you're not suffering as a result of your practice, you can know that you are on a better way to free yourself from suffering.

18
00:01:45,182 --> 00:01:53,223
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't remove that attitude, whatever you end up doing on the pretext of practice, it's going to be done as a form of cover-up very often.

19
00:01:54,864 --> 00:02:02,465
[SPEAKER_00]: You can also ask yourself whether you're covering things up by, eh... see if you get upset by, eh... when you encounter other people's views.

20
00:02:02,565 --> 00:02:03,485
[SPEAKER_00]: They're different than yours.

21
00:02:04,185 --> 00:02:06,746
[SPEAKER_00]: If you take them personally, if you feel threatened by them.

22
00:02:07,126 --> 00:02:08,786
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are, means you're holding on to your own.

23
00:02:09,626 --> 00:02:12,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if happen... those views happen to be right that you have,

24
00:02:13,073 --> 00:02:14,674
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that you're holding that means you're not right.

25
00:02:14,814 --> 00:02:15,335
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not right.

26
00:02:19,658 --> 00:02:27,484
[SPEAKER_01]: It's very different than, like, expecting a a kind of feeling or a perceptual, like, light.

27
00:02:27,945 --> 00:02:28,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

28
00:02:28,145 --> 00:02:29,006
[SPEAKER_01]: I saw the blue light.

29
00:02:29,126 --> 00:02:29,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

30
00:02:29,426 --> 00:02:31,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, that means I've reached stage one.

31
00:02:31,267 --> 00:02:31,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

32
00:02:31,428 --> 00:02:33,689
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I do next after the stage... the blue stage?

33
00:02:33,930 --> 00:02:34,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

34
00:02:34,750 --> 00:02:36,151
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing like that in the suttas.

35
00:02:36,952 --> 00:02:40,635
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm sure there's plenty of contemporary meditations being taught on those lines.

36
00:02:41,477 --> 00:02:46,658
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you're meditating to get a blue light or whatever you want, by all means, but that's not freedom from suffering.

37
00:02:46,918 --> 00:02:50,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Freedom from suffering lies only in one thing, and that thing is understanding the nature of things.

38
00:02:51,579 --> 00:02:52,959
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, the dhamma.

39
00:02:54,420 --> 00:02:55,540
[SPEAKER_00]: Dhamma means phenomena.

40
00:02:55,960 --> 00:02:56,320
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

41
00:02:56,360 --> 00:02:56,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Means things.

42
00:02:58,281 --> 00:03:01,381
[SPEAKER_00]: The dhamma means knowledge of those.

43
00:03:01,401 --> 00:03:08,163
[SPEAKER_00]: And knowledge not like a informational sort of collecting the data and like laying it all out in a psychological manner.

44
00:03:08,243 --> 00:03:08,443
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

45
00:03:08,583 --> 00:03:09,663
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding the nature.

46
00:03:11,636 --> 00:03:16,880
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature of the arisen things, the things that have nature to manifest, what's their fundamental, universal nature.

47
00:03:17,680 --> 00:03:19,481
[SPEAKER_00]: You understand that fully, you are free from suffering.

48
00:03:19,521 --> 00:03:22,703
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't suffer because you can't appropriate it.

49
00:03:22,723 --> 00:03:27,767
[SPEAKER_00]: And that has nothing to do, even if you never see any special light or have special experiences.

50
00:03:27,927 --> 00:03:31,689
[SPEAKER_00]: As Sariputta said himself, he was the foremost after the Buddha.

51
00:03:32,430 --> 00:03:35,452
[SPEAKER_00]: According to the Buddha himself, he said, Sariputta is the foremost after me.

52
00:03:36,508 --> 00:03:42,250
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... he... he said he couldn't even see a mere goblin, let alone have any other supernatural experience or... or release.

53
00:03:42,810 --> 00:03:46,331
[SPEAKER_00]: It was all clarity of understanding and mindfulness and wisdom, basically.

54
00:03:47,011 --> 00:03:48,892
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why he was the second after the Buddha.

55
00:03:50,692 --> 00:04:01,436
[SPEAKER_00]: While others who had, you know, fly in the air and glide... glide the sun and the moon and see the future lives and past lives and all, there's nothing to do with the freedom from suffering.

56
00:04:02,456 --> 00:04:06,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, it can be a side effect of it for some, but it's completely irrelevant.

57
00:04:10,639 --> 00:04:14,903
[SPEAKER_01]: I suppose that's what people are looking for, a kind of...

58
00:04:14,963 --> 00:04:15,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Novelty.

59
00:04:15,343 --> 00:04:15,884
[SPEAKER_00]: ...external thing.

60
00:04:15,984 --> 00:04:16,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

61
00:04:16,104 --> 00:04:28,436
[SPEAKER_00]: I can show everybody... Well, because people's views are generally... they start by being fully embedded in their conceit and self-centeredness, vanity often, sensuality.

62
00:04:29,337 --> 00:04:34,882
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, you just judge that the new spiritual find, inspiration, you judge with those criteria.

63
00:04:36,146 --> 00:04:41,311
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, what's unique, what's new, what's uncommon, what's lofty in that sense.

64
00:04:44,153 --> 00:04:52,301
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, very often, what can be kind of accessed quickly and be so pleasant, it fully overwhelms me.

65
00:04:53,041 --> 00:04:56,104
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just extension of sensual craving, basically, directed elsewhere.

66
00:04:59,139 --> 00:05:01,260
[SPEAKER_01]: So, should you challenge yourself?

67
00:05:01,680 --> 00:05:08,103
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to see where you're at in the practice, should you put challenges upon yourself, like ascetic practices?

68
00:05:08,883 --> 00:05:09,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely, yeah.

69
00:05:09,503 --> 00:05:10,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Especially if you have none.

70
00:05:11,124 --> 00:05:17,987
[SPEAKER_00]: If your life is not... if your life is quite a comfortable, easy, no physical discomfort, anything like that, absolutely, yeah.

71
00:05:18,527 --> 00:05:19,427
[SPEAKER_00]: You use common sense.

72
00:05:19,507 --> 00:05:23,909
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't challenge yourself to the point of risking your life while you're still unenlightened.

73
00:05:24,189 --> 00:05:28,291
[SPEAKER_00]: It's supposed to say you would wisely avoid a wild elephant, a mad cow, a raging dog.

74
00:05:29,890 --> 00:05:38,017
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean he would go and climb on top of a palace and live sheltered and hidden for the rest of his life.

75
00:05:40,259 --> 00:05:52,029
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think, yeah, I think we addressed it before probably, but as it is the case with most people today, on average, certainly in Europe and America, on average people are soft.

76
00:05:54,190 --> 00:05:55,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Like even those who are kind of

77
00:05:57,780 --> 00:05:59,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Not as wealthy, has to have to work.

78
00:05:59,862 --> 00:06:07,328
[SPEAKER_00]: They still have relatively comfortable lives in comparison to how it used to be back in the day in India.

79
00:06:09,129 --> 00:06:10,911
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so that's something you have to factor in.

80
00:06:11,311 --> 00:06:12,652
[SPEAKER_00]: The threshold from which you begin.

81
00:06:20,859 --> 00:06:24,362
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one of those things that you could challenge yourself with is solitude.

82
00:06:25,826 --> 00:06:26,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure.

83
00:06:26,407 --> 00:06:28,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to be alone.

84
00:06:28,988 --> 00:06:34,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that is a challenge and that is also a necessity.

85
00:06:36,094 --> 00:06:47,142
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people actually congregate and come together and live together and pair up, put up with all sorts of discomforts, because solitude is far more terrifying than anything else.

86
00:06:49,584 --> 00:06:50,524
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not optional.

87
00:06:50,925 --> 00:06:53,807
[SPEAKER_00]: It's something your practice should be going towards.

88
00:06:54,940 --> 00:07:02,163
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha would say, you develop the recognition of pleasure in solitude, basically.

89
00:07:02,963 --> 00:07:07,205
[SPEAKER_00]: You learn how to find solitude valuable and pleasing.

90
00:07:07,225 --> 00:07:07,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, it won't be.

91
00:07:09,566 --> 00:07:12,227
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can only learn that through discernment, through understanding.

92
00:07:13,087 --> 00:07:17,449
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you see the benefits of it, and that's how you see the dangers when the solitude is absent.

93
00:07:19,170 --> 00:07:22,771
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you develop a value, factual value of solitude.

94
00:07:24,882 --> 00:07:26,363
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can a layperson do it?

95
00:07:27,523 --> 00:07:32,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it really depends how far that layperson wants to go.

96
00:07:33,966 --> 00:07:40,629
[SPEAKER_00]: If they're thinking about, you know, practicing towards arahantship, anāgāmī, yeah, you can't be a layperson.

97
00:07:40,929 --> 00:07:46,111
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, theoretically, you can be an anāgāmī's layperson, but there was... Or live like a layperson.

98
00:07:47,051 --> 00:07:48,452
[SPEAKER_00]: Or live like a layperson, sure.

99
00:07:48,472 --> 00:07:49,072
[SPEAKER_00]: You can live like a layperson.

100
00:07:49,092 --> 00:07:50,233
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure, sure, sure.

101
00:07:50,273 --> 00:07:51,153
[SPEAKER_00]: You can do that, but

102
00:07:52,803 --> 00:07:55,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Even back in the Buddha's time, that was... that was not common.

103
00:07:56,506 --> 00:07:57,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Usually, people would go forth.

104
00:07:58,688 --> 00:07:59,569
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh...

105
00:08:00,930 --> 00:08:02,571
[SPEAKER_00]: So, today, it's kind of virtually impossible.

106
00:08:03,011 --> 00:08:07,775
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want to go that far, you would have to give up the... the way of a householder's life.

107
00:08:08,616 --> 00:08:09,237
[SPEAKER_00]: You just can't.

108
00:08:09,957 --> 00:08:20,566
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can... you don't necessarily have to go and become a monk, but you have to live like a renunciate, like somebody who's practicing restraint, lives a celibate life,

109
00:08:21,193 --> 00:08:22,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Secluded from people.

110
00:08:22,794 --> 00:08:30,781
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just secluded by not engaging with them, but living with them, but actually physically, physically removed from people.

111
00:08:30,801 --> 00:08:32,842
[SPEAKER_00]: That is necessary.

112
00:08:33,063 --> 00:08:34,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Certainly for the periods of time.

113
00:08:36,685 --> 00:08:48,635
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, so you have to recognize that if you don't want to leave the life of a layman that you know, then you have to sort of lower down your expectations in terms of how much you can develop.

114
00:08:51,410 --> 00:08:55,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's a lot of people are alone in the city.

115
00:08:55,133 --> 00:08:56,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

116
00:08:56,514 --> 00:08:57,295
[SPEAKER_01]: But they're not alone.

117
00:08:57,555 --> 00:08:57,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

118
00:08:58,156 --> 00:09:00,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

119
00:09:00,938 --> 00:09:03,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Being, being, like, living alone doesn't mean you're actually alone.

120
00:09:04,341 --> 00:09:06,362
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no sign of solitude.

121
00:09:07,283 --> 00:09:20,414
[SPEAKER_00]: You need, you need that, that, that physical distance, at least initially, to discern, exactly, to discern the sign of solitude, to discern, well, the sign of your own mind, in a way, the framework of others as a phenomenon, all that.

122
00:09:20,942 --> 00:09:27,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, jhāna, any sort of establishment of mind you need, you can't just sit in a hall with thirty other people on a retreat.

123
00:09:28,605 --> 00:09:30,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you're all perfectly silent, you are with others.

124
00:09:31,066 --> 00:09:31,707
[SPEAKER_00]: There are others.

125
00:09:31,947 --> 00:09:39,450
[SPEAKER_00]: And that... well, it affects the outlook of your experience at the time when you're present.

126
00:09:40,350 --> 00:09:44,152
[SPEAKER_00]: If you remove yourself from it, then you get to understand the framework of that experience.

127
00:09:44,352 --> 00:09:49,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, yes, you can theoretically be with others and not be affected by that on that existential level.

128
00:09:49,849 --> 00:10:02,517
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I said, we all start with a mistake of ignorance, with a mistake of a vijā that is beginningless and real as such, which means you have to reverse to a degree in order to get to see the nature of things and then you can go back to some of it.

129
00:10:02,877 --> 00:10:08,941
[SPEAKER_00]: But you still wouldn't be able to live as a layman, somebody who has understood it properly, freed himself from sensuality, became an arahant.

130
00:10:11,002 --> 00:10:18,527
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, if you recognize the limitations that you are not willing to give up, as in you don't want to go and become a monk and so on,

131
00:10:20,956 --> 00:10:26,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you just have to, like, measure what you can do in that setup and try and tailor your lay setup.

132
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:33,644
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have to work five days a week, well, the other two days, I don't know, rent a cabin in the middle of the forest and that's where you spend it.

133
00:10:35,145 --> 00:10:41,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't spend the valuable time that you can have in solitude, just socializing with others.

134
00:10:42,730 --> 00:10:47,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Spend it in seclusion, study, if you still need to clarify what you need to do,

135
00:10:49,016 --> 00:10:49,376
[SPEAKER_00]: And so on.

136
00:10:49,697 --> 00:10:51,697
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there's different kinds of solitude.

137
00:10:52,358 --> 00:10:55,839
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a physical seclusion, mental seclusion.

138
00:10:56,179 --> 00:10:57,420
[SPEAKER_01]: So... Oh, yeah, sure, sure.

139
00:10:57,860 --> 00:11:02,782
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need physical seclusion, because the mental seclusion is... That's true.

140
00:11:03,022 --> 00:11:07,104
[SPEAKER_00]: But in order to develop a proper mental seclusion, that can only be done through physical seclusion.

141
00:11:09,785 --> 00:11:16,908
[SPEAKER_00]: And yes, like again, as a lay... if a lay person is not able to have a complete physical seclusion, well,

142
00:11:17,535 --> 00:11:20,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you do the best out of what you can.

143
00:11:20,536 --> 00:11:25,418
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in a sense, if you can't be physically remote from people, well, at least spend your time not engaging with them, if you don't have to engage.

144
00:11:25,818 --> 00:11:26,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't talk to them.

145
00:11:27,699 --> 00:11:29,640
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have to talk, well, then talk the bare minimum.

146
00:11:29,660 --> 00:11:30,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't go further.

147
00:11:32,301 --> 00:11:34,362
[SPEAKER_01]: That's difficult, because it's a form of engagement.

148
00:11:34,682 --> 00:11:35,182
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, yeah.

149
00:11:35,202 --> 00:11:37,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Not talking to the person that's sitting right next to you.

150
00:11:37,783 --> 00:11:38,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

151
00:11:38,343 --> 00:11:38,843
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

152
00:11:38,863 --> 00:11:41,424
[SPEAKER_00]: You're already affected just by being with others.

153
00:11:42,105 --> 00:11:45,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if there is a public agreement between us, that we will not speak.

154
00:11:46,299 --> 00:11:52,184
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still others, presence of others, the assumption of the external point of view to yourself, all that is present.

155
00:11:52,284 --> 00:12:00,151
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just... you do have to be physically removed, some people more, some people less, for periods of time, to develop certain clarity.

156
00:12:01,412 --> 00:12:13,282
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, just pick a suited random, and it is an 80% chance you'll be talking about value of solitude, and forest, remote dwellings, and empty lodgings, and go there, and, you know, don't waste your time.

157
00:12:16,837 --> 00:12:23,542
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's like one of the reasons why a layperson cannot develop these things, it's because the household life is crowded and dusty.

158
00:12:23,562 --> 00:12:26,365
[SPEAKER_00]: It's crowded, it's always people, always people, always things going on.

159
00:12:26,745 --> 00:12:27,586
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't practice them.

160
00:12:28,847 --> 00:12:31,729
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, you must abandon that type of life if you want to practice.

161
00:12:33,090 --> 00:12:39,815
[SPEAKER_00]: And that, again, even if you don't want to go and become a monk.

162
00:12:40,976 --> 00:12:43,398
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, the lifestyle choices matter.

163
00:12:44,919 --> 00:12:45,680
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't, like,

164
00:12:46,205 --> 00:12:49,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Not change any aspect of your lifestyle and expect to develop practice.

165
00:12:50,708 --> 00:12:51,128
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

166
00:12:53,070 --> 00:12:55,491
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the precepts are already a lifestyle choice.

167
00:13:00,195 --> 00:13:09,981
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in terms of looking after yourself, I mean, physical health, what's what's going too far?

168
00:13:10,922 --> 00:13:12,083
[SPEAKER_01]: What's not going far enough?

169
00:13:14,644 --> 00:13:16,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what's going too far?

170
00:13:18,266 --> 00:13:23,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, are we supposed to eat... eat just the bare minimum, just so they can get the... just...

171
00:13:23,970 --> 00:13:24,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, right.

172
00:13:24,630 --> 00:13:25,111
[SPEAKER_00]: I see what you mean.

173
00:13:25,131 --> 00:13:26,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

174
00:13:26,632 --> 00:13:28,513
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you keep on breathing, keep on walking.

175
00:13:30,794 --> 00:13:34,857
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're eating very little, obviously, you start to waste away.

176
00:13:36,198 --> 00:13:42,922
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's... there is no chance a person in that kind of state, going on pīndapāta,

177
00:13:44,016 --> 00:13:44,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, absolutely.

178
00:13:45,617 --> 00:13:46,558
[SPEAKER_01]: In a mountainous area.

179
00:13:46,678 --> 00:13:48,199
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, like, especially for the monks.

180
00:13:48,299 --> 00:13:52,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if they want to live like forest monks, or just in name, but actually in life.

181
00:13:52,022 --> 00:13:53,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Seclusion means far away from other people.

182
00:13:53,883 --> 00:13:55,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Seclusion means effort.

183
00:13:55,064 --> 00:13:56,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Seclusion means exposure to elements.

184
00:13:57,045 --> 00:13:58,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Seclusion means ability to endure.

185
00:13:59,507 --> 00:14:06,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Seclusion means toughness, physical toughness, discomfort, that doesn't bother you, that you find comfortable.

186
00:14:07,873 --> 00:14:08,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, again, as I said,

187
00:14:10,074 --> 00:14:22,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Ultimately, if a person manages to properly develop their mind, properly establish their mind in jhāna and in similar establishments, they will enjoy the roughness, toughness, discomfort even more.

188
00:14:23,762 --> 00:14:33,268
[SPEAKER_00]: But in order to get there, they already need to be able to take a degree of pain without their mind just melting away, without their mindfulness fizzling out the moment that discomfort touches them.

189
00:14:34,049 --> 00:14:35,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is usually why people are afraid.

190
00:14:35,690 --> 00:14:35,930
[SPEAKER_00]: People

191
00:14:36,362 --> 00:14:39,204
[SPEAKER_00]: And I, again, I speak out of personal experiences.

192
00:14:39,224 --> 00:14:43,267
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you get into some meditation, then immediately, oh, I must stop.

193
00:14:43,427 --> 00:14:45,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything, basically, that will result in fatigue.

194
00:14:45,849 --> 00:14:48,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything that will tire me, it will compromise my mindfulness.

195
00:14:48,451 --> 00:14:49,072
[SPEAKER_00]: Or make me sick.

196
00:14:49,712 --> 00:14:50,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Or make me sick, exactly.

197
00:14:50,973 --> 00:14:52,814
[SPEAKER_00]: But... Well, that's the thing.

198
00:14:53,915 --> 00:14:59,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Health, it's the whole... it's the principle that you sort of open this up with.

199
00:14:59,079 --> 00:15:02,302
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the... like when the Buddha said, you can't stop eating because you will die, so...

200
00:15:03,609 --> 00:15:04,209
[SPEAKER_00]: What you do then?

201
00:15:04,249 --> 00:15:06,991
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't blame food for your sensuality, because you weren't there.

202
00:15:08,032 --> 00:15:13,256
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, there are certain things you have to stop doing, such as sexual intercourse, killing others, and so on.

203
00:15:13,876 --> 00:15:15,157
[SPEAKER_00]: That cannot be done wholesomely.

204
00:15:15,658 --> 00:15:22,783
[SPEAKER_00]: But things that are necessary, or things that are helpful, can be determined helpful, whether they are rooted in wholesome intentions.

205
00:15:23,543 --> 00:15:32,870
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're mindful, if you develop discernment, and if you invest effort in clarity of your motivation in regard to the reasons why you're eating food,

206
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:42,908
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha himself said, sometimes he would eat two bowls of food filled to the brim, while he has disciples who live only on a handful of rice every third day or whatever.

207
00:15:44,590 --> 00:15:46,251
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean the Buddha was sensual.

208
00:15:46,571 --> 00:15:49,554
[SPEAKER_00]: It just means that at that time, there was a need for more.

209
00:15:51,035 --> 00:16:00,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, even if he ate ten bowls of food, for no point in that duration of that action of eating would have he been rooted in sensuality,

210
00:16:01,449 --> 00:16:06,570
[SPEAKER_00]: Or in vanity, or desire for physical beauty, so he eats, so he can be healthy and admired by others.

211
00:16:06,630 --> 00:16:06,770
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

212
00:16:07,211 --> 00:16:08,291
[SPEAKER_00]: He ate because he was hungry.

213
00:16:08,591 --> 00:16:08,931
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

214
00:16:10,111 --> 00:16:12,132
[SPEAKER_00]: But that takes... that takes proper mindfulness.

215
00:16:12,212 --> 00:16:15,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you are careless, then you're automatically eating out of sensuality.

216
00:16:16,793 --> 00:16:17,533
[SPEAKER_00]: It's same with health.

217
00:16:17,753 --> 00:16:27,936
[SPEAKER_00]: People get obsessed with their health and with their... whatever... tablets and multivitamins and acupunctures and all of that.

218
00:16:29,486 --> 00:16:31,787
[SPEAKER_00]: That can help when there is a need for that help.

219
00:16:31,927 --> 00:16:35,870
[SPEAKER_00]: But more often than not, people start protecting it just for the sake of it.

220
00:16:36,830 --> 00:16:39,812
[SPEAKER_00]: But health is very much like the immune system.

221
00:16:40,092 --> 00:16:41,733
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll develop it if you challenge it.

222
00:16:42,414 --> 00:16:43,734
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't, you lose it.

223
00:16:45,595 --> 00:16:54,220
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not accidental when you hear like, oh, the old monk in Thailand, 90-something years, walking Pindapar every single day of his life, like four or five kilometers, and lived to the old age fully active.

224
00:16:54,941 --> 00:16:56,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because he built

225
00:16:57,319 --> 00:16:59,159
[SPEAKER_00]: He built, basically, his body adjusted.

226
00:16:59,380 --> 00:16:59,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Resilience.

227
00:17:00,140 --> 00:17:00,680
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

228
00:17:00,740 --> 00:17:05,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Through the exposure, through through fatigue, effort, strength, endurance, all of that.

229
00:17:05,921 --> 00:17:09,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, if your life doesn't require that, you don't have to do it specifically.

230
00:17:09,962 --> 00:17:13,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Although, it would probably be useful to have some sort of physical discipline.

231
00:17:14,363 --> 00:17:15,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Something that will tire you.

232
00:17:15,844 --> 00:17:19,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Something that will make you, make you, make your heart race, basically.

233
00:17:20,865 --> 00:17:26,006
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I don't mean like do spiritual sort of energy flows with your tai chi or some yoga twists and whatnot.

234
00:17:26,600 --> 00:17:30,083
[SPEAKER_00]: You want actually to tire yourself to be able to see.

235
00:17:30,563 --> 00:17:32,925
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you be mindful, whether you're tired or not?

236
00:17:33,986 --> 00:17:35,927
[SPEAKER_00]: How can your mindfulness remain unaffected by that?

237
00:17:36,427 --> 00:17:40,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, if your mindfulness... and again, I'm speaking that our personal experience is a labor.

238
00:17:40,310 --> 00:17:41,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I was afraid to get tired.

239
00:17:41,931 --> 00:17:48,616
[SPEAKER_00]: I stopped doing sports, stopped exercising, because it would disturb my mindfulness, which is because I didn't know what mindfulness was.

240
00:17:48,796 --> 00:17:53,340
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that which I thought was mindfulness, was indeed disturbed by fatigue, by tiredness.

241
00:17:53,820 --> 00:17:54,761
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I must stop that.

242
00:17:55,464 --> 00:17:56,665
[SPEAKER_00]: But it was a complete mistake.

243
00:17:57,185 --> 00:17:58,546
[SPEAKER_00]: I wish somebody told me sooner.

244
00:17:59,567 --> 00:18:03,590
[SPEAKER_00]: But fortunately, like, I didn't end up hurting myself, in terms of physically, as many monks do.

245
00:18:03,610 --> 00:18:08,734
[SPEAKER_00]: When you stop everything, you just sit for a whole day, you don't do anything, and then suddenly, you forget how unfit you are.

246
00:18:09,535 --> 00:18:14,419
[SPEAKER_00]: You go for a massive, long, too long or something, and you dislocate your... your disc slips and whatnot.

247
00:18:14,479 --> 00:18:16,240
[SPEAKER_00]: And that happens when... when body is just weak.

248
00:18:16,941 --> 00:18:19,823
[SPEAKER_00]: You... you lost a certain degree of resilience and strength.

249
00:18:20,445 --> 00:18:21,226
[SPEAKER_00]: And you forgot about it.

250
00:18:21,246 --> 00:18:26,390
[SPEAKER_01]: You go lift a box and that's it, because you've been doing... Yeah, you move a pot plant and that's it.

251
00:18:26,470 --> 00:18:28,151
[SPEAKER_00]: You end up with a chiropractor.

252
00:18:28,171 --> 00:18:29,552
[SPEAKER_00]: How many times that happened to people?

253
00:18:29,732 --> 00:18:31,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, yeah, loads, loads.

254
00:18:32,955 --> 00:18:39,740
[SPEAKER_00]: And all because, just because of that kind of same attitude of, I must not get tired, because it will disturb my mindfulness, and then becomes a habit of not getting tired.

255
00:18:41,021 --> 00:18:41,682
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

256
00:18:41,742 --> 00:18:43,863
[SPEAKER_00]: You want something that will tire you, so that you can see that.

257
00:18:43,923 --> 00:18:49,648
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if your mindfulness is disturbed by the mere fatigue, what are you going to do?

258
00:18:50,406 --> 00:18:58,411
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're facing death, illness, dying of cancer, body shutting down, hurting from every angle, no way to rest from it.

259
00:18:59,872 --> 00:19:00,352
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no way.

260
00:19:00,672 --> 00:19:09,737
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, think how the monks lived back in the Buddha's day, in the edges of Himalayas, like, trekking for for months at end, just to meet the Buddha in time.

261
00:19:10,037 --> 00:19:11,258
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, try.

262
00:19:11,498 --> 00:19:14,420
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, today, if you want to do that, you need, like, military training.

263
00:19:15,443 --> 00:19:20,767
[SPEAKER_00]: Take an average guy or you need to be doing that like gradually increasing for like decades to be able to do it.

264
00:19:22,648 --> 00:19:29,752
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to recognize this necessity and also indifference towards mindfulness, whether you're fatigued or not.

265
00:19:30,953 --> 00:19:32,774
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you're properly mindful, it doesn't matter.

266
00:19:33,755 --> 00:19:40,800
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it won't matter whether you're in a deathbed, struggling to breathe or you're perfectly healthy in full strength and full whatever.

267
00:19:41,380 --> 00:19:41,960
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter.

268
00:19:42,541 --> 00:19:44,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you discern where the mindfulness is.

269
00:19:45,411 --> 00:19:51,393
[SPEAKER_00]: But yeah, if you blame your fatigue, if you blame things you have to do, if you blame work, if you blame exercise, well, good luck with that.

270
00:19:51,753 --> 00:20:00,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can sort of survive like that, if you live in a precious environment, that's usually based on luxury and comfort, even if you don't necessarily recognize it as such.

271
00:20:01,097 --> 00:20:07,580
[SPEAKER_00]: But in, you know, in Sābhāsana Sutta, there's a whole, the Buddha said, there's a whole domain of asavas to be abandoned simply by enduring.

272
00:20:08,480 --> 00:20:13,942
[SPEAKER_00]: Enduring heat, cold, discomfort, tired, hunger, enduring insects, enduring elements, basically.

273
00:20:15,105 --> 00:20:21,707
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're not able to endure it, if your mind folds away from it, just as a prospect of enduring, you're nowhere near it.

274
00:20:24,068 --> 00:20:28,869
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't mean, of course, that people who live, like lay people who live in the cities, oh, I must go and run and live in a mountain.

275
00:20:29,129 --> 00:20:29,809
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not going to work.

276
00:20:30,309 --> 00:20:36,191
[SPEAKER_00]: But you do need some sort of physical discipline, something that will challenge you and tire you and make you tougher and stronger.

277
00:20:36,211 --> 00:20:37,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing too complicated, is it?

278
00:20:37,791 --> 00:20:38,572
[SPEAKER_00]: No, of course not.

279
00:20:38,912 --> 00:20:40,052
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, absolutely not.

280
00:20:40,872 --> 00:20:43,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, you want to have it rooted in those reasons.

281
00:20:44,211 --> 00:20:48,553
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, sure, yeah, people can be doing exercise for all sorts of reasons.

282
00:20:48,813 --> 00:20:54,135
[SPEAKER_00]: For vanity, for wanting to be the best, the quickest, the strongest... That's the number one reason to do it.

283
00:20:54,856 --> 00:20:55,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, sure, sure, sure.

284
00:20:56,016 --> 00:20:56,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

285
00:20:56,336 --> 00:21:01,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's... that's why often people will project, they will think, like, you can't do it for any other reason except the vanity.

286
00:21:03,039 --> 00:21:10,663
[SPEAKER_00]: And then... and they also equally mistakenly project, thinking, in order then to do something that won't be in vanity, it must be something completely different.

287
00:21:10,743 --> 00:21:13,044
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they get doing, like, Tai Chi or things like that.

288
00:21:13,745 --> 00:21:19,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, again, it's a valid form of exercise for some, but it's not the one you're looking after.

289
00:21:19,646 --> 00:21:21,567
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not going to make you tougher or more resilient.

290
00:21:22,767 --> 00:21:27,088
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to look at things that actually are most economical.

291
00:21:27,248 --> 00:21:31,029
[SPEAKER_00]: With the least effort, you get most result, in terms of your exercise.

292
00:21:31,389 --> 00:21:36,710
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't have to spend five hours training and running and whatnot.

293
00:21:36,730 --> 00:21:39,791
[SPEAKER_00]: But you do the minimum, as a form of discipline, as a form of chore,

294
00:21:42,773 --> 00:21:45,256
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it will pertain to everything else in a positive manner.

295
00:21:50,883 --> 00:21:58,393
[SPEAKER_01]: I suppose a lot of the type of work that people used to do, generally, is more physical, you know, farm work and so on.

296
00:21:58,413 --> 00:21:58,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

297
00:21:58,974 --> 00:22:01,437
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not done out of, you know,

298
00:22:02,372 --> 00:22:03,593
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's done out of necessity.

299
00:22:04,753 --> 00:22:08,836
[SPEAKER_00]: As a side effect of that necessity was they were fit and tough and able to endure.

300
00:22:08,996 --> 00:22:12,778
[SPEAKER_01]: But now you have, like, gyms in the cities.

301
00:22:13,018 --> 00:22:13,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

302
00:22:13,318 --> 00:22:17,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is basically set up a luxury.

303
00:22:18,021 --> 00:22:19,702
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's all about mirrors and vanity.

304
00:22:19,722 --> 00:22:20,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

305
00:22:20,322 --> 00:22:20,903
[SPEAKER_00]: I was just thinking that.

306
00:22:21,263 --> 00:22:21,903
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's the thing.

307
00:22:21,983 --> 00:22:26,266
[SPEAKER_00]: If, like, it's not denying the lifestyle has changed.

308
00:22:26,706 --> 00:22:29,948
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't just go and start plowing the land and whatever.

309
00:22:30,853 --> 00:22:32,654
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is absolutely no need for that anymore.

310
00:22:33,034 --> 00:22:39,015
[SPEAKER_00]: But you recognize, like, certain loss with that, which is that, like, physical toughness, unable to endure discomfort and pain.

311
00:22:40,376 --> 00:22:45,157
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to compensate for that, you don't have to change your lifestyle to a great degree.

312
00:22:45,217 --> 00:22:50,298
[SPEAKER_00]: But exactly, you find, you do the training, you do the exercises, they will add up to that which you don't have.

313
00:22:51,199 --> 00:22:56,020
[SPEAKER_00]: And especially, as a monk who is interested to live like a forest monk,

314
00:22:56,618 --> 00:23:03,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Who is interested to live solely on Pindaparth in the remote dwellings, frequenting the forest and stuff.

315
00:23:03,444 --> 00:23:04,105
[SPEAKER_00]: If you just...

316
00:23:04,125 --> 00:23:10,050
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, we had that here happens, like when people were like 15 years younger than us, came and stayed here for a couple of days.

317
00:23:10,250 --> 00:23:11,831
[SPEAKER_00]: By the third day, they were dead.

318
00:23:12,292 --> 00:23:12,872
[SPEAKER_00]: They were broken.

319
00:23:13,793 --> 00:23:16,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Just having to walk five kilometers for their food.

320
00:23:17,676 --> 00:23:18,637
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like done every day.

321
00:23:19,077 --> 00:23:19,678
[SPEAKER_00]: And no problem.

322
00:23:20,198 --> 00:23:21,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Almost look forward for it.

323
00:23:22,488 --> 00:23:23,188
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you just...

324
00:23:23,208 --> 00:23:28,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Even, even the old ladies who live nearby... My goodness, yeah, yeah, the tea-picking ladies, yeah, yeah, exactly.

325
00:23:28,792 --> 00:23:33,475
[SPEAKER_00]: The tea-picking... Yeah, 70-year-old, just held steep like this.

326
00:23:33,555 --> 00:23:37,137
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know, probably 15 kilos of tea leaves in the bag on her back.

327
00:23:37,937 --> 00:23:42,040
[SPEAKER_00]: And he's there like a, like a mountain deer, just not a slip, nothing.

328
00:23:42,060 --> 00:23:43,561
[SPEAKER_00]: She's been doing it her whole life.

329
00:23:43,621 --> 00:23:44,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Out of anything.

330
00:23:44,741 --> 00:23:45,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

331
00:23:45,362 --> 00:23:46,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, that's the thing.

332
00:23:46,322 --> 00:23:47,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody who hasn't been doing it,

333
00:23:49,048 --> 00:23:55,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody who hasn't been doing it, in order to do it, more often than not, it's like vanity is the reason.

334
00:23:57,595 --> 00:24:00,458
[SPEAKER_00]: And in a way, that's fine, if that's what makes you do it.

335
00:24:00,578 --> 00:24:03,320
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you just change the reason and keep doing it.

336
00:24:04,141 --> 00:24:11,387
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for somebody who lives in the city, if you want to go and start, I don't know, endurance training or strength training, go to a gym that doesn't have the mirrors.

337
00:24:11,727 --> 00:24:12,588
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the first thing.

338
00:24:12,808 --> 00:24:16,311
[SPEAKER_00]: There's absolutely no reason for lifting in front of a mirror, except vanity.

339
00:24:17,702 --> 00:24:24,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Even actually, even in terms of the form, and developing the proper lifting form, for example, in strength training, you should not look in the mirror.

340
00:24:24,943 --> 00:24:26,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the... the... what was that?

341
00:24:26,564 --> 00:24:27,624
[SPEAKER_00]: The Russian Olympic team.

342
00:24:27,704 --> 00:24:35,405
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know, maybe they still do it, but back in the day, they used to lift this complex, like Olympic snatches, and power cleans, and so on, blindfolded.

343
00:24:36,285 --> 00:24:38,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you want to learn the motorics, you want to learn the movement.

344
00:24:39,426 --> 00:24:45,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, the compound exercises, no mirror, strength, endurance, repetition, and increasing the weight.

345
00:24:46,114 --> 00:24:53,460
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you want to look... if you want to develop a muscle bulging out, so that you'd be attracting... You cannot do that for wholesome reasons.

346
00:24:53,540 --> 00:24:54,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

347
00:24:54,160 --> 00:24:54,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

348
00:24:55,001 --> 00:25:02,507
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you being fit is a result of your discipline and basically pursuit of physical toughness and endurance, that's absolutely fine.

349
00:25:02,827 --> 00:25:05,269
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense as you being healthy as a result of eating.

350
00:25:06,350 --> 00:25:06,850
[SPEAKER_00]: That's fine.

351
00:25:07,591 --> 00:25:11,734
[SPEAKER_00]: Just don't... because you can't slip into unwholesome accidentally.

352
00:25:12,254 --> 00:25:13,275
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't accidentally

353
00:25:13,829 --> 00:25:15,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, start eating for pleasure.

354
00:25:16,210 --> 00:25:16,350
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

355
00:25:16,851 --> 00:25:19,252
[SPEAKER_00]: You do it by deciding to go for pleasure.

356
00:25:19,952 --> 00:25:24,134
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't accidentally go and start training with weights and accidentally do bodybuilding.

357
00:25:24,194 --> 00:25:24,354
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

358
00:25:25,455 --> 00:25:33,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Bodybuilding is all about, basically, developing proportions, developing size, which means it's all about the way you look, which means it's all about the vanity.

359
00:25:34,960 --> 00:25:42,884
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, if you don't do any form of that, if you actually... if you actually do the strength endurance training, then you wouldn't need more than what?

360
00:25:43,569 --> 00:25:55,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Half an hour, three times a week, using some proper strength, like routines, proven, been around for like 40 years, and you wouldn't have to spend like six hours jogging and running and everything else.

361
00:25:57,014 --> 00:25:59,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just that you'll be healthy, you'll also be able to take the pain.

362
00:26:00,716 --> 00:26:11,820
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't shy away from discomfort, you wouldn't shy away from physical fatigue, and nor would your mental composure be affected by it, because you trained yourself.

363
00:26:13,424 --> 00:26:17,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, or just take a backpack, go in the mountains, and walk, and live in a tent.

364
00:26:19,446 --> 00:26:21,607
[SPEAKER_00]: And you'll get used to it, and you get tougher, you get stronger.

365
00:26:22,907 --> 00:26:37,132
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, back in the day, the olden days... Yeah, but back in the day, like, there's no, it's not, it's, it's not accidental that the Buddha kept referring to, like, you know, living, like, trying to inspire monks with, like, similes of warriors, and, and soldiers, and toughness.

366
00:26:37,232 --> 00:26:40,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because it was like that.

367
00:26:40,213 --> 00:26:40,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine just

368
00:26:41,357 --> 00:26:51,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, carrying a backpack of 5 kilos, let alone 15 or 20 kilos, and then hike for 20 kilometers on uneven terrain, that's a military training.

369
00:26:51,865 --> 00:26:56,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Today, that's considered almost like, what, a military elite infantry.

370
00:26:57,509 --> 00:26:58,550
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, that was your lifestyle.

371
00:26:59,571 --> 00:27:03,534
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't even get to sleep in a nice place, you get to sleep under a root of a tree.

372
00:27:04,895 --> 00:27:08,598
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean, like, people might not think they live a luxurious life,

373
00:27:09,383 --> 00:27:19,505
[SPEAKER_00]: But in comparison, it's very... Like, even here on the hill, where we do walk Pindapur every single day, five to six kilometers, up and down the mountain, rain, wind, doesn't matter.

374
00:27:21,166 --> 00:27:23,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Overall, it's actually quite comfortable up here.

375
00:27:24,226 --> 00:27:26,487
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's quite easy.

376
00:27:27,347 --> 00:27:30,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you do want to have the ultimate perspective in your mind.

377
00:27:30,928 --> 00:27:33,329
[SPEAKER_00]: How did the root of a tree-dweller live?

378
00:27:34,209 --> 00:27:38,950
[SPEAKER_00]: You think when they walked across the entire Bihar to meet the Buddha, they were just flying in the air.

379
00:27:39,497 --> 00:27:47,781
[SPEAKER_00]: No, they're walking, carrying, carrying the ball, which were probably heavy cast iron ball, and you think a weak person can do that?

380
00:27:47,821 --> 00:27:48,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Kettles, pots.

381
00:27:49,162 --> 00:27:55,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Kettles, pots, umbrellas, thick felt, those felt blankets they used to use in India for rain and stuff.

382
00:27:55,545 --> 00:27:57,886
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like 15, 20 kilo of weight on you.

383
00:27:58,986 --> 00:28:00,067
[SPEAKER_00]: Sun, heat.

384
00:28:00,787 --> 00:28:01,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry?

385
00:28:01,607 --> 00:28:02,688
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, they did not have.

386
00:28:02,948 --> 00:28:03,828
[SPEAKER_00]: What was the name of that?

387
00:28:04,669 --> 00:28:06,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Baba, Baba tent.

388
00:28:06,210 --> 00:28:07,710
[SPEAKER_00]: A thousand pounds.

389
00:28:08,331 --> 00:28:09,111
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, a thousand pounds.

390
00:28:09,739 --> 00:28:11,360
[SPEAKER_00]: 800 gram tent or something.

391
00:28:11,400 --> 00:28:12,181
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

392
00:28:12,321 --> 00:28:16,165
[SPEAKER_00]: There are massive wall blankets, canvases, bowls and...

393
00:28:16,766 --> 00:28:20,830
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just just picture that and picture that doing... you doing that every day.

394
00:28:21,251 --> 00:28:23,593
[SPEAKER_00]: And on top of that, that's your practice.

395
00:28:23,753 --> 00:28:25,175
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, that's how you also practice.

396
00:28:26,716 --> 00:28:27,297
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

397
00:28:27,317 --> 00:28:32,162
[SPEAKER_00]: People are a bit too precious, in a sense, including myself, where I started.

398
00:28:32,603 --> 00:28:40,328
[SPEAKER_00]: You think, oh, I stole this, nothing must challenge me, because this is... Yeah, you start like that, but you have to see that that's not where the problem is.

399
00:28:40,929 --> 00:28:46,973
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is not in your exercise, the problem is not in your eating, taking medicine, paying attention to your health.

400
00:28:47,893 --> 00:28:53,077
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is you doing those things for wrong reasons, which means it's your reasons that make it a problem or not.

401
00:28:54,818 --> 00:28:59,521
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the same sense, as I was just saying, if you want to go and develop solitude, you have to be able to take it.

402
00:29:01,477 --> 00:29:04,699
[SPEAKER_00]: And that will often require, yeah, the degree of physical discomfort.

403
00:29:04,959 --> 00:29:05,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Independence.

404
00:29:06,540 --> 00:29:07,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Independence, yeah, yeah.

405
00:29:09,422 --> 00:29:10,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Getting wood, chopping wood.

406
00:29:11,002 --> 00:29:11,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

407
00:29:11,583 --> 00:29:17,846
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't have, like, twenty servants, twenty junior monk servants around you, just, just attending to your every wish.

408
00:29:18,106 --> 00:29:18,246
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

409
00:29:18,927 --> 00:29:20,528
[SPEAKER_00]: You want solitude, you have to do it yourself.

410
00:29:22,009 --> 00:29:24,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you have to be able to do it yourself.

411
00:29:24,630 --> 00:29:27,832
[SPEAKER_00]: And you won't be able if your body is simply not strong enough.

412
00:29:28,252 --> 00:29:29,153
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't, you can't do it.

413
00:29:29,173 --> 00:29:29,953
[SPEAKER_00]: You just can't take it.

414
00:29:31,501 --> 00:29:33,602
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't get out of bed after one day's work.

415
00:29:34,403 --> 00:29:35,003
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

416
00:29:35,023 --> 00:29:43,068
[SPEAKER_00]: In that sense, exercise can start approximating that for those who don't work like that, who don't have that toughness developed through work and so on.

417
00:29:45,830 --> 00:29:46,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, good cardio.

418
00:29:47,911 --> 00:29:54,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, get your heart pumping, whether through lifting weights or whether through running, if you can, even treadmills and so on.

419
00:29:54,835 --> 00:29:57,537
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I said, don't get obsessed about it either.

420
00:29:58,814 --> 00:30:02,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Do a research, see which one is the most economical.

421
00:30:03,015 --> 00:30:05,776
[SPEAKER_00]: By that I mean, the least you can do, the most you get from it.

422
00:30:07,196 --> 00:30:10,337
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, I think there's a universal consensus.

423
00:30:10,397 --> 00:30:22,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Of those who know, of those who don't identify weights with bodybuilding, who know the difference, of like professional athletes, it's like, you know, heavy barbell training, kettlebells, bodyweight, pull-ups and things like that.

424
00:30:23,020 --> 00:30:27,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that will, you know, increase your bone densities, joint strength, stability, all of that.

425
00:30:28,290 --> 00:30:33,331
[SPEAKER_00]: You can do your tai chi if you want in the spare time, but you actually want to stress the body in a controlled manner.

426
00:30:33,851 --> 00:30:35,211
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how it gets tougher, stronger.

427
00:30:35,991 --> 00:30:43,913
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you can eat one meal a day, and go and climb the mountain, and carry a 20 kilo backpack, and sleep on a rock, like a baby.

428
00:30:47,513 --> 00:30:57,235
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know, you know that difference even, you know, out of like our own experience, when you, when you, you live in Europe, or some well sort of well-developed

429
00:30:57,837 --> 00:30:58,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Temple.

430
00:30:59,138 --> 00:31:04,340
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you know, you like even going to Thailand and staying in some more rural temples, coming to Sri Lanka.

431
00:31:04,360 --> 00:31:13,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Or it's just that, yeah, people who were not prepared physically, well, they didn't last long.

432
00:31:13,123 --> 00:31:18,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, those who did, had to go through a lot of pain very quickly, because they just haven't been ready for it.

433
00:31:21,467 --> 00:31:24,528
[SPEAKER_00]: And above all, people end up feeling it, feeling the prospect

434
00:31:25,497 --> 00:31:32,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Our physical discomfort, challenge, endurance, fatigue, even without actually engaging in it.

435
00:31:33,461 --> 00:31:34,982
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, that's something we've seen many times.

436
00:31:35,623 --> 00:31:39,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Especially in the monasteries, they were about work, lots of work and war projects.

437
00:31:40,065 --> 00:31:45,748
[SPEAKER_00]: When people had to work, you just see the genuine, genuine terror in their eyes, because it's just going to be overwhelmed.

438
00:31:45,828 --> 00:31:49,250
[SPEAKER_00]: The mindfulness is going to go, and they'll think about sensuality, and then they might disrobe.

439
00:31:50,192 --> 00:31:51,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that should worry you.

440
00:31:51,473 --> 00:31:54,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the fact that you might have to work, or get tired, or exercise.

441
00:31:54,855 --> 00:31:58,997
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that doing that will push you all the way back into sensuality.

442
00:31:59,137 --> 00:32:00,258
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the problem there.

443
00:32:01,719 --> 00:32:04,520
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can avoid it initially, until you start developing yourself.

444
00:32:04,560 --> 00:32:08,923
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you never make the effort to develop yourself in the right manner, you can't avoid it forever.

445
00:32:09,103 --> 00:32:12,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as I said, once your health starts to fail, strength is relative.

446
00:32:13,565 --> 00:32:19,789
[SPEAKER_00]: For an old person, body failing, what used to be weakness, it is now strength, in comparison.

447
00:32:21,590 --> 00:32:23,151
[SPEAKER_01]: So, lifestyle choices matter.

448
00:32:25,112 --> 00:32:26,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't take your health for granted.

449
00:32:27,072 --> 00:32:28,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't take your strength for granted.

450
00:32:28,953 --> 00:32:31,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't take your freedom from illness for granted.

451
00:32:31,714 --> 00:32:38,778
[SPEAKER_00]: But actually, start emulating and preparing yourself for when those things go, for when those things start challenging you physically, which will then challenge you mentally.

452
00:32:40,819 --> 00:32:45,401
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can take it, then you can take your mind even further above it, and really not be affected.

453
00:32:45,961 --> 00:32:50,863
[SPEAKER_00]: But as the Buddha said, again, in Śrīmad-Bhāgavatam, there's a whole set of taints, a whole set of asuras,

454
00:32:51,645 --> 00:32:57,089
[SPEAKER_00]: To be abandoned purely by enduring, by toughing them out.

455
00:32:59,791 --> 00:33:01,773
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that's something that's often missing today.

456
00:33:03,154 --> 00:33:05,236
[SPEAKER_00]: People are way too spiritual, so to speak.

457
00:33:07,337 --> 00:33:08,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Even their exercise becomes spiritual.

458
00:33:10,380 --> 00:33:12,702
[SPEAKER_00]: And it doesn't actually pertain to the physical aspect anymore.

459
00:33:13,763 --> 00:33:15,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Certainly lifestyles become too spiritual.

460
00:33:16,785 --> 00:33:18,026
[SPEAKER_00]: No manual labor, nothing.

461
00:33:20,386 --> 00:33:32,175
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you pay attention to the... if you're interested in practice, you pay attention to the lifestyle choices, the choices you make, not just for today and tomorrow, but in the long run as well, and factor that in.

462
00:33:32,395 --> 00:33:42,182
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're interested in practice and developing the environment that will support your solitude and so on, pay attention to preparing your body physically to be able to do it.

463
00:33:42,662 --> 00:33:46,805
[SPEAKER_00]: It's easy to keep the vanity out, if you're careful.

464
00:33:47,546 --> 00:33:48,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Avoid looking yourself in the mirror,

465
00:33:50,967 --> 00:33:55,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Do the compound lifts or general type of exercises.

466
00:33:55,330 --> 00:33:56,350
[SPEAKER_00]: They will stress your body.

467
00:33:57,451 --> 00:34:01,553
[SPEAKER_00]: Not necessarily result in some developing of proportions and whatnot.

468
00:34:02,734 --> 00:34:04,595
[SPEAKER_00]: But you do need to start stressing yourself.

469
00:34:05,276 --> 00:34:11,219
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, in the same manner, you can't expect to just jump into solitude from a busy life.

470
00:34:11,299 --> 00:34:12,880
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can be forced into it.

471
00:34:13,080 --> 00:34:17,523
[SPEAKER_00]: You can allow yourself to be forced into it, which is usually how those popular going-to-retreats work.

472
00:34:18,480 --> 00:34:21,101
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you get all these real energy releases and whatnot.

473
00:34:21,181 --> 00:34:23,083
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's because of your lifestyle beforehand.

474
00:34:25,844 --> 00:34:36,370
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't go for that, you want to start exposing yourself to that type of stress, gradually, but pretty much steadily.

475
00:34:37,390 --> 00:34:38,631
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not optional, basically.

476
00:34:41,212 --> 00:34:45,695
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize what the goal is, which is to be able to find joy and happiness,

477
00:34:46,145 --> 00:34:54,573
[SPEAKER_00]: And to prefer the actual physical solitude and absence of other people, and then to develop yourself for that, physically and mentally.

478
00:34:54,593 --> 00:34:57,455
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how mentally do you stress your mind, mentally?

479
00:34:58,817 --> 00:35:07,925
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, by being alone, deciding to be alone, and not engaging in any distraction, not answering any phone calls, don't going to see anyone, for the periods of time.

480
00:35:09,166 --> 00:35:09,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, no distraction.

481
00:35:10,147 --> 00:35:12,009
[SPEAKER_00]: No distraction, of course, yeah.

482
00:35:14,318 --> 00:35:26,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, as I said, if you are relatively fit, physically, can take some pain and toughness, yeah, take a backpack and go for like three or four day hike in a little tent.

483
00:35:26,284 --> 00:35:28,525
[SPEAKER_00]: It will give you a sense of how you used to be.

484
00:35:29,225 --> 00:35:30,506
[SPEAKER_00]: And practice in that environment.

485
00:35:30,526 --> 00:35:35,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't just endure and put up with elements, but actually take your mind above it.

486
00:35:37,750 --> 00:35:44,495
[SPEAKER_01]: In terms of, like, just having a minor headache, you know, little headache, instant pain killer.

487
00:35:44,575 --> 00:35:45,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure.

488
00:35:45,456 --> 00:35:47,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Instant relief seeked immediately.

489
00:35:47,337 --> 00:35:49,799
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you just don't take the pain killer... Oh, yeah, yeah.

490
00:35:50,519 --> 00:35:55,242
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize what... how you want to live and take responsibility for it.

491
00:35:55,783 --> 00:36:04,209
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you can take medicine when you're ill, for sake of staying healthy, not out of fear for getting sick.

492
00:36:04,809 --> 00:36:05,770
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you will eat,

493
00:36:06,362 --> 00:36:10,584
[SPEAKER_00]: For the sake of staying alive and healthy, not for beautification or fear of death.

494
00:36:10,604 --> 00:36:10,764
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

495
00:36:12,065 --> 00:36:15,907
[SPEAKER_01]: You took responsibility for it.

496
00:36:16,988 --> 00:36:21,630
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you'll exercise, in order to be able to take the pain even more, without being afraid of it.

497
00:36:27,913 --> 00:36:30,295
[SPEAKER_01]: And just to deal with the simple dwelling, you know.

498
00:36:30,435 --> 00:36:30,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

499
00:36:31,732 --> 00:36:36,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Taking you away from the elements, but not overly protecting you from...

500
00:36:36,636 --> 00:36:37,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

501
00:36:39,198 --> 00:36:41,099
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you need a thick soft mattress?

502
00:36:41,640 --> 00:36:42,020
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

503
00:36:42,240 --> 00:36:43,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the body is not used to sleep on hard.

504
00:36:44,041 --> 00:36:44,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it not used to?

505
00:36:44,802 --> 00:36:46,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you never exposed it to.

506
00:36:46,323 --> 00:36:47,684
[SPEAKER_00]: You never stressed it in a manner.

507
00:36:49,986 --> 00:36:52,348
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not, it's not empty ascetic practices.

508
00:36:53,308 --> 00:36:56,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's empty or not, it's whether you don't do it for the right reasons or not.

509
00:37:01,769 --> 00:37:10,715
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the utmost, most inspiring ascetic performance can be more empty than eating and exercising, if you do it for the wrong reasons.

510
00:37:12,677 --> 00:37:22,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you do eating and exercising for the right reasons, the reasons that you understand, the reasons that will pertain to your freedom from suffering, through your effort, yeah, that's what makes it wholesome.

511
00:37:23,344 --> 00:37:27,347
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're just going self-mortifying yourself for just the reason of whatever,

512
00:37:28,206 --> 00:37:32,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Proving to yourself that you do it, crushing it, or whatever, without any grain of reflection or insight.

513
00:37:33,989 --> 00:37:35,389
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, in that sense, it's completely wrong.

514
00:37:36,150 --> 00:37:41,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, eh... being, eh... skin and bones means I'm on the right path.

515
00:37:41,472 --> 00:37:42,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, exactly, yeah, yeah.

516
00:37:42,953 --> 00:37:43,293
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't.

517
00:37:45,014 --> 00:37:46,574
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, there were... there were that suttas as well.

518
00:37:46,614 --> 00:37:50,536
[SPEAKER_01]: The, eh... that, eh... King...

519
00:37:50,556 --> 00:37:52,417
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know if it's Pasenadi or the other one.

520
00:37:52,857 --> 00:37:54,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Anyway, he went to visit the Buddha.

521
00:37:54,078 --> 00:37:56,999
[SPEAKER_00]: He was staying in this kuti, and on the way to the kuti, he was walking to that park.

522
00:37:57,619 --> 00:38:04,164
[SPEAKER_00]: But all these young and healthy monks were doing walk meditation, like pacing from one tree to the other, back and forth, and they were quite happy.

523
00:38:04,804 --> 00:38:05,745
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, wow, look at that!

524
00:38:06,185 --> 00:38:07,426
[SPEAKER_00]: This teaching is so wonderful.

525
00:38:07,446 --> 00:38:08,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he praised the Buddha.

526
00:38:08,267 --> 00:38:10,088
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha said, what you see, praise the word in me.

527
00:38:10,108 --> 00:38:21,897
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, well, for example, just now, this teaching is so wonderful when you have like men in their youth, still fully able to access sensual pleasures, enjoy life, carry it all up, and they're happy about it.

528
00:38:24,979 --> 00:38:25,940
[SPEAKER_00]: They're very content.

529
00:38:26,563 --> 00:38:33,025
[SPEAKER_00]: While you go to the other ascetics and brāhmaṇas, they're all dried up, skin and bone, just hating on everyone, miserable, unhappy.

530
00:38:38,367 --> 00:38:41,508
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to start discerning the reasons behind your actions.

531
00:38:42,509 --> 00:38:49,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether that's those actions are aimed at health, life, strength, lifestyle in general.

532
00:38:50,332 --> 00:38:53,073
[SPEAKER_00]: You are responsible for making them wholesome or unwholesome.

533
00:38:53,233 --> 00:38:53,693
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not.

534
00:38:54,585 --> 00:38:56,126
[SPEAKER_00]: necessarily determined by themselves.

535
00:38:56,206 --> 00:38:56,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Very few are.

536
00:38:58,527 --> 00:39:01,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Like non-celibacy, of course, that's always unwholesome.

537
00:39:02,028 --> 00:39:04,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Killing, harming, lying, stealing, that's always unwholesome.

538
00:39:05,309 --> 00:39:11,552
[SPEAKER_00]: But pretty much everything else, it's going to be unwholesome if you do it for unwholesome reasons.

539
00:39:11,592 --> 00:39:13,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if everybody else thinks it's wholesome.

540
00:39:13,552 --> 00:39:18,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's wholesome to sit in a dhamma hall with 50 people, in silence, that's that's noble and beautiful.

541
00:39:18,514 --> 00:39:18,835
[SPEAKER_00]: It isn't.

542
00:39:19,135 --> 00:39:19,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Often it isn't.

543
00:39:23,074 --> 00:39:25,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Because often people end up doing it because they're still afraid to be alone.

544
